                <h2>Step 1: Build a Unified Fund Management Dashboard</h2>
<p>Enterprise advertising usually spans multiple markets, platforms, currencies, and ad accounts. Operators should not rely on remaining balance alone. They also need to know whether each account can support upcoming spend and whether budgets are pacing as planned.</p>
<p>Organize the dashboard around three metric groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Liquidity metrics:</strong> Track available balance, frozen funds, pending settlements, and estimated days of runway. Available balance rate can be calculated as available balance divided by total funded amount. Estimate runway using the average daily spend from the previous seven days.</li>
<li><strong>Budget execution metrics:</strong> Compare daily and monthly budgets with planned and actual spend. A useful pacing variance is actual spend divided by planned spend, minus elapsed time progress. If actual spending consistently exceeds the planned pace, do not immediately increase the budget. First check tracking issues, audience expansion, and rising costs.</li>
<li><strong>Account health metrics:</strong> Monitor payment failures, review status, rejection rates, rejected creatives, and abnormal accounts stuck in the learning phase. An account with sufficient funds but restricted delivery still represents a media risk.</li>
</ul>
<p>Standardize currencies, time zones, tax treatment, and billing periods. Organize records by platform, country, account, currency, and owner. A consolidated summary should never replace account-level detail.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Run Daily Monitoring and Tiered Alerts</h2>
<p>Schedule two checks each day: one for immediate operational risks and another for overnight consumption.</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Check balances and payment methods</strong><br />
   Flag accounts likely to run out within seven days, those likely to run out within three days, and balances below the minimum safety line. Confirm whether automatic top-ups are active and whether the payment method remains valid. High-balance accounts should also be checked for failed charges or unsettled funds.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Review spending pace</strong><br />
   For accounts materially ahead of schedule, inspect attribution, duplicate conversions, targeting scope, bidding strategy, and audience overlap. If the deviation is temporary, adjust daily spend limits first rather than increasing the total budget.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Investigate account and creative issues</strong><br />
   Watch for sudden increases in rejection rates, repeated learning resets, and sharp declines in delivery. Confirm technical, review, and policy causes before changing creatives, adjusting budgets, or moving funds. Changing several variables simultaneously makes it harder to identify the actual cause.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Document resolution</strong><br />
   Every alert should include the trigger time, affected account, owner, action taken, expected recovery time, and verification result. An alert is not closed until it has an owner and a deadline.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>Alert thresholds should reflect historical volatility. For example, estimate safety balances as a multiple of average daily spend and calibrate them against the previous 30 days of fluctuations instead of applying one absolute threshold to every account.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 3: Prioritize Recharges Based on Burn Rate</h2>
<p>Separate funds into three categories: working capital required for normal delivery, flexible funds for controlled campaigns, and reserves for account review or payment issues.</p>
<p>Rank recharge requests by forecast demand, safety balance, account importance, funding time, exchange rates, and fees. Prioritize accounts that are close to exhaustion and have stable delivery. For accounts with volatile spend or rising costs, control the budget before adding funds to avoid idle balances.</p>
<p>Maintain a recharge ledger with order number, requested amount, actual payment, fees, funding time, destination account, and operator. In approval-based workflows, separate request, review, and payment roles to reduce duplicate submissions and unauthorized actions.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Reconcile Spend and Review Performance Monthly</h2>
<p>Compare platform consumption with internal invoices daily, resolve small differences weekly, and report by platform, account, and currency each month. Use consistent gross or net tax treatment, and disclose refunds, rebates, promotional credits, and foreign-exchange gains or losses separately.</p>
<p>After one cross-border e-commerce team introduced shared identifiers for recharges, automatic charges, and manual adjustments, finance could trace every platform expense to an ad account. This reduced cases where platform delivery appeared without a corresponding internal record. The value of the system comes from traceability, not from adding more spreadsheets.</p>
<h2>Weekly Review Checklist</h2>
<p>Confirm the following every week:</p>
<ul>
<li>High-spend accounts have at least seven days of visible balance runway;</li>
<li>Budget pacing anomalies have been classified as technical or strategic issues;</li>
<li>Recharge records include fees, funding time, and reviewer;</li>
<li>Multi-account access follows the principle of least privilege;</li>
<li>Platform statements, internal spend, and finance postings have been reconciled and variances reviewed.</li>
</ul>
<p>The objective of fund management is not to maintain high balances in every account. It is to ensure that every advertising budget has a clear plan, traceable destination, and controlled risk.</p>
